# DELIBERATELY NOT engine-strict. Read this before adding it back.
#
# v2.51.1 set `engine-strict=true` here, and the reasoning was sound at the
# time: the updater that runs is always the code you are upgrading FROM, so a
# host below the Node floor executes its own updater, which knows nothing about
# the floor, and installs Node-22 dependencies onto Node 20 regardless. This
# file arrives in the working tree via `git reset --hard` BEFORE `npm install`,
# so it was the one guard that reached every updater including ones written
# before the floor existed. Measured then and still true: `engines` alone is
# advisory, and npm installs anyway without this.
#
# What that guard could not do is FIX anything. It converted "installs broken
# dependencies" into "cannot update at all, ever, until someone ssh's in" — and
# a host in that state re-runs the update, fails identically, and stays stuck.
# That happened in production: three consecutive releases landed their files on
# a Node 20 host and none of them could complete, because the guard blocked the
# only step that would have let the old updater reach its own restart.
#
# scripts/safe-boot.sh (v2.55.1) now reconciles the runtime on every boot: it
# compares the running Node major against `engines.node` and raises it before
# starting the app. That is a guard that repairs instead of refusing, and it
# runs from the tree the failed update already delivered.
#
# So the sequence now completes unattended. Measured on node:20.20.2 against the
# real dependency tree: `npm install --omit=dev` exits 0 and installs 123
# packages. The old updater therefore finishes, reaches `process.exit(0)`, and
# systemd re-execs — at which point safe-boot.sh raises Node to 22 and the app
# starts on a correct runtime. Also measured: better-sqlite3 built under Node 20
# loads cleanly under Node 22 (N-API is ABI-stable), so nothing needs rebuilding
# across the upgrade.
#
# The window this reopens is one boot: dependencies installed under Node 20 and
# running under Node 20, for the seconds between the updater exiting and
# safe-boot.sh finishing. If safe-boot.sh cannot raise the runtime (no apt, or
# no root and no passwordless sudo) and the new release genuinely cannot run on
# the old one, the boot crashes and the sentinel rolls back to the previous SHA
# — the same protection engine-strict was providing, arrived at without
# stranding every host that simply needed a newer Node.
#
# `engines.node` in package.json stays. It is what safe-boot.sh reads to learn
# the floor, and npm still prints EBADENGINE as a warning.
